\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Training dynamics.} The SDF head's mean absolute error decreases from \placeholder{X.X}\,cm (step 500) to \placeholder{X.X}\,cm (step 30,000).

\paragraph{Inference latency.} \ours{} in zero-overhead mode adds no latency. In optional verification mode (retaining the SDF head), overhead is ${\sim}$5\,ms. By contrast, AEGIS adds \placeholder{XX}\,ms and OmniGuide adds \placeholder{XX}\,ms per step (Appendix~\ref{app:compute}).

\paragraph{Deployment complexity.} Beyond latency, \ours{} eliminates the auxiliary infrastructure that inference-time methods require. AEGIS demands a runtime CBF solver, real-time SDF queries, open-set object detection, and depth-map processing---four additional modules, each a potential failure point. OmniGuide requires a second forward pass through an SDF guidance network, differentiable kinematics, and online guidance field computation. \ours{} requires nothing beyond the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ forward pass and the same RGB inputs as the unmodified base model: no SDF server, no depth reconstruction, no constraint solver. Each additional runtime module increases the deployment failure surface; by internalizing safety into weights, \ours{} reduces the system to a single inference call with no external dependencies.



%=============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
%=============================================================================

This paper presented \ours{}, a training-time method that embeds geometric collision awareness directly into VLA flow-matching weights through SDF auxiliary supervision and a noise-aware collision-shaping loss. Unlike prior approaches that impose safety at inference time through guided sampling \citep{song2026omniguide}, post-hoc trajectory correction \citep{hu2025aegis}, or constrained reinforcement learning \citep{zhang2025safevla}, \ours{} produces a model whose standard forward pass generates inherently collision-aware actions with zero additional latency, memory, or runtime geometry acquisition at deployment---the model requires only the same RGB observations as the base VLA, eliminating the need for SDF fields, meshes, or depth maps. This architectural simplicity translates directly to deployment robustness: whereas inference-time methods introduce multiple auxiliary modules (SDF servers, constraint solvers, depth pipelines) that each constitute an independent failure point, \ours{} reduces the safety-critical path to the model's own forward pass.

Our results demonstrate \placeholder{XX\%} reduction in collision rate with only \placeholder{X.X} percentage points of task success degradation across 32 collision scenarios.

The ``train-with, infer-without'' paradigm is not specific to collision avoidance: the same framework could internalize other auxiliary objectives (energy efficiency, human comfort zones) by substituting the SDF head with a corresponding prediction target. Several directions remain open: neural SDF estimation from depth observations for real-world deployment, evaluation on additional VLA backbones (Octo, RT-2), and combining \ours{}'s geometric shaping with constrained RL \citep{zhao2024scpo,zhang2025safevla} for multi-step safety guarantees.

\subsection*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Single benchmark and backbone.} All experiments were conducted on LIBERO/SafeLIBERO with $\pi_{0.5}$. Validating on additional benchmarks (ManiSkill, RoboCasa) and VLA architectures (Octo, RT-2) would strengthen generality claims.

\paragraph{Known geometry assumption.} Ground-truth SDF labels require analytical scene geometry (MuJoCo). Real-world deployment will need neural SDF estimation from depth or point clouds, and the effect of label noise on collision shaping remains uncharacterized.

\paragraph{Simulation only.} All results are simulated; a real-robot validation is needed to confirm that internalized collision awareness survives the sim-to-real gap.

\paragraph{Binary collision definition.} SafeLIBERO defines collision as binary contact (any contact constitutes a collision). For tasks requiring push-through-obstacle motions or compliant contact, the collision penalty may over-suppress necessary interactions---a general limitation of collision-free safety paradigms. Future work could address this via task-conditioned safety margins or force-aware collision thresholds.

\subsection*{Broader Impact}

A broader impact discussion is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:broader_impact}.


